From 8ecf537e73a4eb4f5c1defb081ceb29f0f215f58 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Magnus Reimann <magnus.reimann@mailbox.org>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 19:17:40 +0100
Subject: [PATCH gnumach 21/22] vm: do not let page flags under different locks
 share a memory location

The flags of struct vm_page are protected either by the page queues
lock (inactive, active, laundry, external_laundry, free, reference,
external) or by the object lock (busy, wanted, tabled, fictitious,
private, absent, error, dirty, precious, overwriting), but they all
are bit-fields of the same unsigned int.  Adjacent bit-fields form a
single memory location, and the compiler updates a flag with a
read-modify-write of it.  On a multiprocessor, a processor holding
only the object lock can thus write back a stale value of a flag a
processor holding the page queues lock just changed, and conversely.

This was caught by gdb on a 2-processor Debian GNU/Hurd amd64 guest
running Chromium.  The pageout thread, balancing segments, pulls an
active page in vm_page_seg_pull_active_page (clearing page->active),
fails to lock its object, which another processor holds, and puts the
page back, which fails with

  vm_page_seg_add_active_page: Assertion
  `!page->free && !page->active && !page->inactive' failed

because page->active got set again in between.

Separate the two kinds with a zero-width bit-field, which makes them
distinct memory locations.  wire_count, only changed with both locks
held, stays with the page queues flags.  The size of struct vm_page
doesn't change.

* vm/vm_page.h (struct vm_page): Separate the flags protected by the
page queues lock from those protected by the object lock.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 vm/vm_page.h | 8 ++++++++
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)

diff --git a/vm/vm_page.h b/vm/vm_page.h
index 6990f95e..863ee71e 100644
--- a/vm/vm_page.h
+++ b/vm/vm_page.h
@@ -97,6 +97,13 @@ struct vm_page {
 	vm_object_t	object;		/* which object am I in (O,P) */
 	vm_offset_t	offset;		/* offset into that object (O,P) */
 
+	/*
+	 * The flags protected by the page queues lock (P) and those protected
+	 * by the object lock (O) must not share a memory location: updating
+	 * a bit-field may rewrite the whole location, and on a multiprocessor,
+	 * holding only one of the locks, undo a concurrent update of a flag
+	 * of the other kind.  The zero-width bit-field separates them.
+	 */
 	unsigned int	wire_count:15,	/* how many wired down maps use me?
 					   (O&P) */
 	/* boolean_t */	inactive:1,	/* page is in inactive list (P) */
@@ -106,6 +113,7 @@ struct vm_page {
 			free:1,		/* page is on free list (P) */
 			reference:1,	/* page has been used (P) */
 			external:1,	/* page in external object (P) */
+			:0,
 			busy:1,		/* page is in transit (O) */
 			wanted:1,	/* someone is waiting for page (O) */
 			tabled:1,	/* page is in VP table (O) */
